Subject: runtime: poll WASIX's epoll with the wasix build tag
Last-Update: 2026-10-04

The wasip1 port polls with WASI preview 1's poll_oneoff, which takes its
whole subscription list on every call and cannot be woken by another
thread. With the wasix tag the poller is WASIX's epoll, which is
Linux's: an interest list the runtime keeps, so that a descriptor
registered by one thread is watched by the poll another thread is
blocked in, and an event descriptor netpollBreak writes to wake that
poll. Sockets are registered edge-triggered as on Linux; netpollarm has
nothing to arm and netpoll.go is unchanged.

The wakeup descriptor is written once per netpollBreak, and the flag
netpollWakeSig skips further breaks until a blocking netpoll reads it.
Linux reports a level-triggered descriptor to every wait while it stays
readable; WASIX's epoll reports an event descriptor once per write, so
a nonblocking netpoll(0) running while a wakeup was pending would take
the only report, the blocking poll would never see it, the flag would
stay set, and every timer in the program would stop once all Ms were
idle. A nonblocking poll that takes the report writes the descriptor
again, and the descriptor is non-blocking as Linux's is, so a read that
finds the count already drained fails instead of sleeping outside the
poll.

